**Race timeline.** The bug appears because start order and finish order differ. State needs an explicit generation number rather than trusting whichever Promise settles last.

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
  participant UI
  participant Old as request 1: c
  participant New as request 2: cat
  UI->>Old: start token 1
  UI->>New: abort old, start token 2
  New-->>UI: finish first; token matches; commit
  Old-->>UI: late finish; token stale; ignore
```

**Runnable simulation.** The request counter protects state even though the older operation finishes last.

```javascript
const delay = (ms, value) => new Promise((resolve) => {
  setTimeout(() => resolve(value), ms);
});
let activeRequest = 0;
let shown = null;

async function search(query, ms) {
  const requestId = ++activeRequest;
  const result = await delay(ms, query.toUpperCase());
  if (requestId === activeRequest) shown = result;
}

const oldRequest = search("c", 25);
const newRequest = search("cat", 5);
await Promise.all([oldRequest, newRequest]);
console.log(shown); // CAT
```

**Boundary.** Never assume cancelling a write request reverses the server operation. For mutations, use idempotency keys or version checks in addition to controlling which response updates the client.

